WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.333
The printing press was incredibly revolutionary in all sorts of ways.

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:09.030
Nobody talks about like that 50-75 year period when the printing press first came about and

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:15.719
it was just like, madness, and revolution, and insanity, and all sorts of crazy things happened.

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:20.069
And it took awhile for society to sort of
come to terms with the fact that like, okay,

00:00:20.069 --> 00:00:24.789
the monopoly that there used to be on printing,
is now gone.

00:00:24.789 --> 00:00:27.729
And I think we're going through something
of the same thing right now.

00:00:27.729 --> 00:00:32.790
Following last week’s attack on the US
Capitol by a pro-Trump mob, Twitter permanently

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:36.800
banned the president of the United States
from its platform, and Facebook and YouTube

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.650
suspended his accounts.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:43.650
Meanwhile Parler, which marketed itself as
a more open alternative to Twitter, was booted

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:49.140
from the Apple and Google app stores, and
Amazon Web Services evicted the company from

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.240
its cloud computing platform.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.900
Is the great deplatforming of 2021 a threat
to free speech?

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.280
Or should we “think of Twitter as a Christian
bakery and Trump as a gay wedding cake,”

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.632
as one user of the platform quipped, meaning
that nobody should be able to force a private company

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:08.706
to do business with someone it disagrees with?

00:01:08.706 --> 00:01:13.920
Enter Mike Masnick, the 46-year-old entrepreneur
and analyst behind the influential website,

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.479
Techdirt, and the digital think tank, the Copia
Institute.

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:21.759
Where others are constantly talking about
how to restrict and regulate the internet

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:27.260
and tech giants to conform to one ideological
vision or another, Masnick champions protocols

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.960
and practices that he thinks would lead to
a more decentralized internet and culture,

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:38.100
including expanding Section 230, widespread
encryption, and tools that give end users,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.450
rather than political and cultural commissars, even more power to control what we say

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:44.719
and see online.

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:49.819
Reason spoke with Masnick about what current
debates over social media get drastically wrong,

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:54.975
how free speech is simultaneously empowered and imperiled by politicians here and abroad,

00:01:54.975 --> 00:01:59.754
and why a more-decentralized internet
is not just possible but preferable

00:01:59.754 --> 00:02:02.110
 to what we have now.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.779
Mike Masnick, thanks for talking to Reason.

00:02:04.779 --> 00:02:06.349
Yeah, thanks for having me.

00:02:06.349 --> 00:02:08.840
Alright, we're going to talk about Section
230.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.963
We're going to talk about deplatforming, replatforming,
unplatforming, and eventually

00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:21.135
we're going to get to talk about your fantastic 2019 essay
for the Knight Foundation,

00:02:21.135 --> 00:02:22.733
"Protocols, Not Platforms,"

00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:29.090
which is about getting to a decentralized or a more decentralized internet.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:34.883
But first, let me start off with something
that you wrote recently after—you know,

00:02:34.883 --> 00:02:41.130
you know I say recently, last week seems like a year
ago, you know, and I don't know, you know,

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.281
like 2010 seems like a thousand years ago.

00:02:44.281 --> 00:02:49.423
But you wrote last week, I guess I think on
Friday or something, when Trump had been kicked off

00:02:49.423 --> 00:02:55.820
at this point of Facebook and of Twitter, you wrote, Trump is perhaps the perfect example

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:01.390
of why demanding clear rules on social media
and how they moderate is stupid.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:02.882
What do you mean by that?

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:08.427
Yeah, I mean, it's you know, there's a lot of context that goes into that and it's part of a larger discussion.

00:03:08.427 --> 00:03:17.287
But I think, you know, almost everybody who is coming at this debate with solutions that say

00:03:17.287 --> 00:03:22.270
this is right, this is wrong, or anything
along those lines generally hasn't thought

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.017
these issues through. 

00:03:24.017 --> 00:03:30.659
And hasn't realized not that these decisions are difficult, but that  they are impossible.

00:03:30.659 --> 00:03:35.937
And this is something that I've sort of tried to drive home through various conversations that I've had,

00:03:35.937 --> 00:03:41.183
that, you know, for people who
have to make moderation decisions.

00:03:41.183 --> 00:03:44.670
They have a bunch of choices in front of them, including do nothing.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.390
And all those choices are bad.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:49.520
All those choices have consequences.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.590
And so there is no right answer.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:56.652
And I think that a lot of people and this is across the political spectrum, in all sorts of different ways, 

00:03:56.652 --> 00:04:01.661
and with all sorts of different backgrounds, people think that there is a sort of correct way to do it, 

00:04:01.661 --> 00:04:06.092
whether that's nothing—like if we get the the algorithm right, you know,

00:04:06.092 --> 00:04:09.845
and we won't say the formula, but the algorithm correct, 

00:04:09.845 --> 00:04:13.320
we just have to put it into, you know, inserted into the internet

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.884
and then everything will be ok.

00:04:15.884 --> 00:04:17.964
It gets beyond that, right? There's certainly a lot of that.

